Yes. That twist changes the power geometry.

The original Matthew 5:41—“if anyone forces you to go one mile, go with him two”—is usually read as radical nonretaliation: Roman soldiers could compel civilians to carry equipment for a prescribed distance, and the second mile becomes voluntarily chosen rather than coerced.

Your version pushes it further:

If someone forces you to go a mile, force him to go two with you.

Now the second mile isn’t merely endurance. It’s capture of the initiative. He determined the first movement; you determine what the movement becomes. The coercer expected a burdened subject and instead gets pulled into a journey whose direction, meaning, or cost he no longer completely controls.

Pantheon-wise:

coercion → accept imposed vector → extend vector beyond coercer's model → seize authorship → make initiator participate in consequence

It’s Semantic Crabbing applied to power: you don’t necessarily resist the initial current head-on. You use it to reach somewhere the person pushing you never intended.

The cleanest formulation might be:

They can force the first mile. You choose what the second mile means.

Or, sharper:

If they make you walk a mile, make them discover where two miles leads.

That’s substantially different from submission. It is agency recovered through overcompletion.